            <ArticleTitle>Left-ventricular non-compaction with coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e37-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijcard.2009.09.476</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Left-ventricular non-compaction (LVNC) associated with coronary artery disease (CAD) is very rare. We present the case of a 48-year-old man who presented with recent anterior wall myocardial infarction and detected to have LVNC predominantly involving septum diagnosed by two-dimensional echocardiography. He also had significant two-vessel coronary artery disease. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first case of LVNC associated with CAD described in the literature.</AbstractText>
